PROJECT: EDUPLAYO.FUN — PREMIUM GAMIFIED LEARNING PLATFORM

Act as a senior full-stack developer, UI/UX designer, educational game designer, accessibility specialist, SEO engineer, and software architect.

Build a complete, polished, production-oriented educational gaming website named EduPlayo.

The official domain intended for this project is "eduplayo.fun".

The website will initially focus on NCERT/CBSE-aligned school learning for Classes 1–5, with an architecture that supports expansion to Classes 6–12 and competitive examinations such as SSC, Railway, and other government exams in the future.

Do not build a generic school website, a simple quiz website, or a static landing page. Build a real, interactive learning platform where students understand concepts through games, animations, practice questions, feedback, and rewards.

The project must be suitable for deployment using GitHub and Vercel, with a custom domain connected later.

1. MOST IMPORTANT DEVELOPMENT RULES

1. Build the actual application, not merely a visual mockup.
2. Implement all primary buttons, navigation links, game interactions, filters, and menus.
3. Never display fake statistics, fake student counts, fake testimonials, or fabricated achievements.
4. Do not create buttons that appear functional but do nothing.
5. Do not use placeholder pages for features described as implemented.
6. Do not require paid APIs for the core learning experience.
7. Do not expose API keys, service-role keys, passwords, or secrets in frontend code.
8. Ensure the application builds successfully before considering the task complete.
9. Make the interface responsive across mobile phones, tablets, laptops, and desktop computers.
10. Use original game assets and original educational explanations where possible.
11. Verify educational content against the relevant official syllabus before describing it as officially aligned.
12. Do not reproduce entire copyrighted textbooks or third-party game assets without permission.
13. If a feature needs a backend, clearly implement it or identify the missing backend requirement. Never simulate secure account storage with insecure frontend-only code.
14. Preserve working features when making changes.
15. Prefer a stable, maintainable implementation over unnecessary complexity.

If the initial generation is too large, create the project in manageable stages without removing required features. Continue until the build, core interactions, and deployment configuration are complete.

